decisions
---------

ADR-001: Collection and analysis are separate concerns.

    Context

        Collecting evidence and interpreting evidence have different
        responsibilities.

    Decision

        collector performs collection only.

        Technology detection, entity extraction, business logic inference,
        vulnerability analysis and reporting belong to downstream tools.

    Consequences

        collector remains focused on producing evidence.

        New analysis techniques can be introduced without changing collector.


ADR-002: The crawl frontier is explicit.

    Context

        Recursive browser automation hides crawl state and makes traversal
        difficult to inspect and extend.

    Decision

        collector maintains an explicit work queue.

        Page URLs are represented as work items.

    Consequences

        Crawl state is explicit.

        Queueing and processing remain separate concerns.

        Traversal strategies can evolve without restructuring browser
        operations.


ADR-003: Page discovery is based on navigable links.

    Context

        collector needs to discover additional pages without treating every
        browser resource as crawl work.

    Decision

        collector discovers pages from HTTP and HTTPS links in the document.

        URL fragments are removed before URLs enter the crawl frontier.

        Only URLs permitted by the configured scope are queued.

    Consequences

        Page traversal remains separate from resource observation.

        Fragment variants of the same URL do not create separate crawl work.

        Query strings remain significant and may therefore produce separate page
        visits.


ADR-004: Browser-observed resources are captured from responses.

    Context

        Earlier designs discovered script and stylesheet URLs from the DOM and
        subsequently fetched those URLs separately.

        This introduced an additional HTTP client into collection and could
        preserve a resource merely because it was referenced rather than because
        the browser actually received it.

    Decision

        collector observes browser responses for scripts and stylesheets.

        The response body is captured directly from the browser.

        collector does not independently fetch discovered resource URLs.

    Consequences

        Preserved artifacts represent resources actually observed by the
        browser.

        Resource capture remains part of the browser navigation lifecycle.

        collector does not generate additional requests for these artifacts.


ADR-005: Shared browser request configuration belongs to the browser context.

    Context

        collector may need to configure behavior that should apply consistently
        to requests generated by the browser.

        Applying such configuration to individual navigations would not cover
        resources and other requests generated by the page.

    Decision

        collector applies shared request headers at the browser-context level.

        The configuration exposes these headers as the headers field.

        The browser context is created explicitly and pages are created from
        that context.

    Consequences

        Configured headers apply consistently to navigation and
        browser-generated resource requests.

        Request configuration remains separate from individual navigation
        operations.

        Additional context-level browser configuration can be introduced
        without expanding the navigation interface.


ADR-006: Rate limiting applies to page navigations at the run level.

    Context

        collector may need to respect a rate limit imposed by the target
        application or collection environment.

        A browser navigation can generate many HTTP requests for documents,
        scripts, stylesheets and other resources. Rate limiting each of those
        requests individually would make the collection rate dependent on the
        application's resource loading behavior.

    Decision

        collector applies rate limiting to intentional page navigations.

        The rate limit is shared across the entire collection run.

        Redirects and browser-generated resource requests are part of the
        navigation that caused them and are not individually rate limited.

        The rate is configured through rateLimit.requestsPerSecond.

        The rate limiter starts with a full bucket.

        Targets and hostnames do not have separate rate limiters.

    Consequences

        The collection rate is predictable independently of how many resources
        an application loads for each page.

        Multiple targets in one run share the same rate limit.

        collector does not need to infer relationships between targets or
        hostnames.

        The configuration author is responsible for choosing an appropriate
        run-level rate.


ADR-007: Browser responses are recorded separately from artifacts.

    Context

        Browser responses contain useful metadata even when their bodies are not
        preserved as artifacts.

    Decision

        collector records every browser response in responses.jsonl.

        Response records contain the resource type, URL and response headers.

        Artifact preservation remains selective and is handled separately.

    Consequences

        Response metadata is available for downstream analysis independently of
        artifact preservation.

        collector can observe responses without unnecessarily storing their
        bodies.

        Response observations and artifact storage remain separate concerns.


ADR-008: Artifact storage is content-addressed.

    Context

        The same script or stylesheet may be encountered by multiple pages.

        Storing identical bytes repeatedly wastes space, while removing repeated
        observations would lose provenance.

    Decision

        Artifact files are named using the SHA-256 hash of their bytes.

        Identical bytes are stored only once within a run.

        artifacts.jsonl records every observed artifact occurrence.

    Consequences

        Physical artifact storage is deduplicated.

        Artifact provenance is preserved.

        Each run remains self-contained.


ADR-009: Structured output is versioned.

    Context

        Output formats will evolve over time.

    Decision

        Structured outputs include an explicit schema version.

    Consequences

        Older collection data remains interpretable.

        Downstream tools can evolve without ambiguity.


ADR-010: Scope is enforced before page processing.

    Context

        collector may encounter links outside the intended collection scope.

        Scope therefore needs to act as a collection boundary rather than merely
        an analysis filter.

    Decision

        Configured scope rules are evaluated before initial targets enter the
        queue and before discovered page URLs are queued.

    Consequences

        Out-of-scope pages are not intentionally navigated by collector.

        Scope remains separate from page discovery and browser operations.
